 "mechanism_cards": {
  "catd10": {
   "axis": "CONCENTRATION",
   "value": "catd10",
   "variant_id": "h33-h33-2-b2-CONCENTRATIONcatd10-20261024-c313194a",
   "hypothesis": "Newly mapped faults are mostly new geometry of existing systems, so the number of new-fault pixels per km^2 tracks the density of already-mapped structure. R0 spreads one dot every ~3 px uniformly over the H19-5 ridge mask, which carries 0.41 dots per km^2-scale block regardless of how much known structure the block holds; moving 10% of the dots from the lowest-density 1 km blocks to the highest raises kernel credit where truth pixels are most numerous.",
   "mechanism": "DTI = T / (0.2T + 0.2(S-M) + 0.8|G|) at fixed S. Moving a dot from a block with near-zero known-structure density to one with 3x the regional density raises the expected max-kernel credit it earns (k(d) = 1 - d/3) and simultaneously raises M, shrinking the wasted-mass term S-M. Both terms push the ratio up; no dot count, mass, spacing or catalogue-distance invariant changes.",
   "non_fault_mimic": "Mapping effort: the catalogue is denser where access, exposure and basin-hosted basin-range normal faults made 1:250k-1:24k compilation easy, not necessarily where 2020s geothermal-focused mapping found new faults. If new truth is instead concentrated in poorly mapped uplands (lidar/surface-imaging targets), this prior is anti-correlated with truth and the variant loses.",
   "predicted_sign_and_size": "MODEL:+0.013 (range [-0.013,+0.040], MODEL; rule = midpoint of the own-prior and other-prior projections)",
   "falsifier": "A live receipt delta <= -0.01 vs R0 falsifies the known-structure-density prior at 1 km and says the catalogue is a mapping-effort artefact; |delta| < 0.01 is no evidence either way (public chunks only).",
